<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhou dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1046–256 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zhou dynasty was the longest-ruling royal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which overthrew the Shang around 1046 BCE and nominally ruled until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> ended it in 256 BCE. Its kings granted lands to kinsmen and allies as hereditary states and justified their rule by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>. After the western capital fell in 771 BCE, the Eastern Zhou kings lost real power to the states of the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zhou dynasty" width="318" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhou_dynasty_1000_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Chinese tradition the Zhou lineage began with Qi, the Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a> (Houji), a culture hero credited with improving agriculture. Later chiefs settled at Bin and then moved to Zhou in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley, which remained their heartland. Wen moved the capital to Feng, and his son Wu, with his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-ziya">Jiang Ziya</a>, led an army of 45,000 men and 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and defeated the Shang king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/di-xin">Di Xin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muye">Battle of Muye</a>. The Zhou made a member of the Shang royal family Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and appear to have spoken a language much like the Shang's.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>King Wu died early, leaving a young heir, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> assisted his nephew King Cheng. When the Three Guards, Zhou princes stationed in the east, rebelled with Shang partisans, the Duke put them down and extended the kingdom eastward. He set up the fengjian system of hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and expounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>. Over generations the kinship ties between the kings and the regional lords thinned, and peripheral states gained power on par with the Zhou.</p><p>In 771 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a>, joined by the Marquis of Shen, whose daughter King You had demoted as queen, sacked the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haojing">Haojing</a> and killed You. Nobles declared the marquis's grandson King Ping and moved the capital east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a>. Royal authority then declined. The court recognised Han, Zhao and Wei as independent states in 403 BCE, and in 344 BCE Duke Hui of Wei was the first to claim the title of king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> captured Wangcheng in 256 BCE and killed King Nan, traditionally the last Zhou king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a> held that Heaven gave power to the one ruler who upheld harmony, and withdrew it from a king whose people suffered. The Zhou used it to justify replacing the Shang. Farming was done mainly by ordinary rural households owing labour, taxes and military service, with slaves a minority. Five ranks of nobility stood below the king: duke, marquis, count, viscount and baron.</p><p>The Zhou period is often seen as the high point of Chinese bronze casting, and in the Eastern Zhou the Hundred Schools of Thought arose, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>, Mohism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a>. The ritual system called li was later set down in texts compiled under the Han.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhou dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The eastern court extracted around 100,000 rolls of silk annually from the Northern <b>Zhou dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Qi was founded shortly after the <b>Zhou</b> conquest of the Shang, conventionally dated to 1046 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Empress Wu Zetian (r. 690–705) interrupted the Tang line by proclaiming her own <b>Zhou dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Zhou dynasty</b>, which overthrew the Shang around 1046 BCE, long inscriptions were cast in bronze ritual vessels, recording royal gifts, appointments and victories; the longest has nearly five hundred characters.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Neuk-do Island shell midden, of the Late Mumun and Early Iron Age, held a few iron objects and Lelang and Yayoi pottery, showing that local societies were drawn into closer contact with the Late <b>Zhou dynasty</b>, the Final Jōmon and the Early Yayoi.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mumun-culture">Mumun culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The establishment of the Eastern <b>Zhou dynasty</b> in 770 BCE began a gradual decline of the Chinese political order; Zhou authority was challenged by local fiefdoms acting as states in their own right, and by the early 5th century the monarchy had become largely symbolic, leaving the field…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
